# Datris Platform - Environment Variables
# Copy this file to .env and fill in your values:
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# Set ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, or the AZURE_OPENAI_* trio. On first
# boot, vault-init.sh seeds the AI primary, codegen, and embedding secrets
# based on which key you provided. Web search (Configuration → AI Providers)
# reuses the same keys — no separate seeding required.
#
# FIRST-BOOT SEED ONLY: the AI provider/key/model values here are used to seed
# Vault the first time it initializes. After that, Vault persists on disk and
# the Configuration UI is the source of truth — edits there stick across
# restarts. Changing these values in .env later has NO effect; change AI config
# in the UI instead. To re-seed from .env, delete the vault-data volume
# (`docker compose down` then `docker volume rm <project>_vault-data`).
# (Non-AI vars below — JAVA_OPTS, USE_*, TAP_RUNNER_*, etc. — are read by the
# container at every boot as usual, not seeded into Vault.)
#
# HIGHLY RECOMMENDED: Anthropic with the latest coding model for CodeGen.
# Tap script generation, AI DQ rules, AI transformations, and NL→SQL all
# depend heavily on the model's coding ability — Claude produces dramatically
# better results than smaller/cheaper models.
#
# HIGHLY RECOMMENDED for embeddings: OpenAI text-embedding-3-small for the
# most reliable, low-cost path — works on any host. For a fully-local
# zero-cost setup, Ollama with bge-m3 is a strong open-source alternative.
# See EMBEDDING_PROVIDER below to mix-and-match (e.g. Claude for chat plus
# OpenAI for embeddings when the bundled embedder is too heavy for your host).
#
# Anthropic path: ai-primary and codegen use Claude. Embedding falls back to
# the bundled TEI sidecar serving bge-m3 (a strong open-source embedding
# model, 1024 dimensions) — no OpenAI key needed for vector destinations.
#
# OpenAI path: all three secrets use OpenAI from the same key.

ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
OPENAI_API_KEY=

# Azure OpenAI path: all three values are required together (AI_PROVIDER=azure).
# ENDPOINT is your resource base URL; MODEL is your chat deployment's NAME
# (name deployments after their model, e.g. gpt-5-2, so model-specific
# handling applies). Embeddings default to the bundled bge-m3 service; see
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=azure below to use an Azure embedding deployment instead.
# AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY=
# AZURE_OPENAI_ENDPOINT=https://YOUR-RESOURCE.openai.azure.com
# AZURE_OPENAI_MODEL=
# Keyless Azure auth (Entra ID) — for resources with disableLocalAuth, or to
# avoid stored keys. Leave AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY unset and either set the
# service-principal trio below, or set nothing at all when the server runs on
# Azure compute with a managed identity. The identity needs the "Cognitive
# Services OpenAI User" role on the resource.
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Amazon Bedrock path: Claude through your AWS account. EXPLICIT-ONLY —
# AI_PROVIDER=bedrock is required; AWS keys alone never select Bedrock (they
# are routinely set for S3 destinations). The AWS keys are optional: leave
# them unset to use the server's IAM role / default credential chain (the
# role needs bedrock:InvokeModel, plus bedrock:ListFoundationModels and
# bedrock:ListInferenceProfiles for the model dropdown). BEDROCK_MODEL is an
# invokable model id — anthropic.-prefixed, or a cross-region inference
# profile (us.anthropic....) where the model isn't on-demand in your region.
# Embeddings default to the bundled bge-m3 service (Bedrock embeddings are
# not supported).
# AI_PROVIDER=bedrock
# AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# AWS_REGION=us-east-1
# BEDROCK_MODEL=anthropic.claude-fable-5

# Grok (xAI) path: ai-primary and codegen use Grok through xAI's OpenAI-compatible
# API. XAI_API_KEY comes from console.x.ai. Embeddings default to the bundled
# bge-m3 service (xAI has no embeddings API).
# XAI_API_KEY=
# GROK_MODEL=grok-4.6

# Optional explicit chat-provider override. Pin a choice when multiple keys
# are present, or when a stray shell env var (e.g. a global OPENAI_API_KEY in
# your shell rc) would otherwise leak in and silently flip the default on
# every rebuild. When unset, vault-init picks whichever key is present
# (OpenAI wins ties, preserving the historical default; azure and grok are only
# auto-picked when they hold the sole key; bedrock is never auto-picked).
# AI_PROVIDER=anthropic

# Optional model overrides (defaults shown)
# ANTHROPIC_MODEL=claude-opus-5
# OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-5.5
# GROK_MODEL=grok-4.6
# CODEGEN_MODEL=claude-opus-5   # or gpt-5.5 if using OpenAI, grok-4.6 for Grok;
#                               # for Azure this is a deployment name (defaults
#                               # to AZURE_OPENAI_MODEL)

# Model the Assistant falls back to when Anthropic returns sustained
# `overloaded_error` for an Opus call (after 2 retries at 1s + 3s backoff).
# Heavy Opus requests (many tools + extended thinking + streaming) sit in
# Anthropic's most-load-shed lane, so a one-shot downgrade to Sonnet for
# the current request keeps the conversation moving instead of surfacing
# the error. The UI shows a small inline notice when the fallback fires.
# Only consulted when the current model is an Anthropic Opus variant —
# OpenAI-only and other-provider deployments can ignore this entirely.
# Bump this when a newer Sonnet ships so the fallback stays current
# without a recompile.
# ANTHROPIC_OVERLOAD_FALLBACK_MODEL=claude-sonnet-4-6

# Optional embedding provider override (decoupled from AI_PROVIDER).
# By default the embedding slot follows AI_PROVIDER: anthropic→bundled TEI
# (bge-m3, 1024-dim), openai→OpenAI text-embedding-3-small (1536-dim).
# Set EMBEDDING_PROVIDER to mix-and-match — e.g. Anthropic for chat/codegen
# and OpenAI for embedding when you don't want to run the bundled TEI sidecar.
# Valid values: openai, azure, tei, ollama. EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai requires
# OPENAI_API_KEY; EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=azure requires AZURE_OPENAI_API_KEY plus
# an embedding deployment on your resource (EMBEDDING_MODEL = its deployment
# name, default text-embedding-3-small). EMBEDDING_MODEL overrides the default
# model for the chosen provider; EMBEDDING_ENDPOINT overrides the URL for
# tei/ollama/azure.
# EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai
# EMBEDDING_MODEL=text-embedding-3-small
# EMBEDDING_ENDPOINT=

# ============================================================================
# Database / store selection (install-time database selection).
# The installer writes these for you; edit + `docker compose up -d` to change
# your mind later. Disabling removes the container but keeps its data volume —
# re-enabling restores the data.
#
# EXISTING-SERVICE gates are opt-OUT with a default of ON (set in
# docker-compose.yml as ${VAR:-1}), so an .env without these lines keeps
# running everything — upgrades can never silently drop a service.
# POSTGRES_ENABLED=1    # 0 = no bundled Postgres container (destination + pgvector)
# TEI_ENABLED=1         # 0 = no bundled embedding server (use EMBEDDING_PROVIDER=openai)
#
# NEW opt-IN services activate via compose profiles (comma-separated):
# qdrant, weaviate, chroma (vector stores) and kafka (local test broker —
# zookeeper + kafka + kafka-ui; production Kafka should be external).
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=qdrant,kafka
# NOTE: `docker compose down` ignores inactive profiles — for a full teardown
# use `docker compose --profile "*" down`.

# External stores (FIRST-BOOT SEED ONLY, like the AI keys above: vault-init
# seeds each secret create-if-absent; afterwards manage credentials in
# Configuration → Secrets). Set these to point Datris at services you already
# run instead of the bundled containers.
#
# External Postgres (pair with POSTGRES_ENABLED=0):
# POSTGRES_JDBC_URL=jdbc:postgresql://db.example.com:5432
# POSTGRES_USER=datris_svc
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
#
# Vector stores — seeded only when HOST is set. For a bundled profile service
# use its compose service name as the host (e.g. QDRANT_HOST=qdrant); for a
# managed cloud instance use its real hostname.
# QDRANT_HOST=      QDRANT_PORT=6334      QDRANT_API_KEY=
# WEAVIATE_HOST=    WEAVIATE_PORT=8079    WEAVIATE_API_KEY=
# CHROMA_HOST=      CHROMA_PORT=8000
# MILVUS_HOST=      MILVUS_PORT=19530     MILVUS_API_KEY=
#
# Kafka producer secret — seeded only when set. Bundled test broker:
# kafka:9092 (with COMPOSE_PROFILES=kafka). External: your broker list.
# KAFKA_BOOTSTRAP_SERVERS=
#
# Snowflake destination credentials (Platform secret `snowflake`) — seeded
# only when SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT is set. privateKey (key-pair auth) recommended
# over password. Warehouse/database/schema are per-pipeline settings, not here.
# SNOWFLAKE_ACCOUNT=xy12345.us-east-1
# SNOWFLAKE_USER=
# SNOWFLAKE_PRIVATE_KEY=
# SNOWFLAKE_PASSWORD=
#
# Databricks destination credentials (Platform secret `databricks`) — seeded
# only when DATABRICKS_HOST is set. Service-principal OAuth (clientId +
# clientSecret) recommended over a personal access token. Warehouse ID and
# Unity Catalog name are per-pipeline settings, not here.
# DATABRICKS_HOST=dbc-a1b2c3d4-e5f6.cloud.databricks.com
# DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID=
# DATABRICKS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# DATABRICKS_TOKEN=
# ============================================================================

# Auth / multi-tenancy toggles (defaults shown — single-tenant OSS).
# Flip USE_USER_AUTH=true to enable username/password login + admin gating
# on the Configuration tab. USE_API_KEYS=true enables per-tenant API-key
# gating on the REST/MCP API. MULTI_TENANT=true switches to per-tenant
# database isolation (intended for hosted/managed deployments).
# See https://docs.datris.ai/user-auth for the full setup walkthrough.
# USE_USER_AUTH=false
# USE_API_KEYS=false
# MULTI_TENANT=false

# Audit log. USE_AUDIT_LOG=true records who created, changed, ran, or deleted
# what — humans by login, agents by API key, the Assistant on the user's
# behalf — readable by admins under Configuration → Audit Log and mirrored to
# the server log for SIEM pickup. AUDIT_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS trims old entries
# (0 = keep forever). See https://docs.datris.ai/audit-log.
# USE_AUDIT_LOG=false
# AUDIT_LOG_RETENTION_DAYS=90

# Hardening (recommended for any internet-reachable deployment):
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true marks the login cookie Secure so it is only sent
#   over HTTPS. Leave unset for local HTTP dev; set true wherever TLS terminates.
# MINIO_WEBHOOK_TOKEN authenticates the MinIO -> /minio-events webhook. Set it to
#   a long random value to close the otherwise-unauthenticated ingestion trigger
#   (MinIO is configured with the same value automatically).
# DATRIS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS=true permits HTTP taps / REST endpoints to call
#   private, loopback, or link-local addresses. Off by default so a tap can't be
#   pointed at cloud metadata or internal services; enable only if your endpoints
#   legitimately live on an internal network.
# SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=true
# MINIO_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=change-me-to-a-long-random-string
# DATRIS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_EGRESS=false

# API keys for accessing Datris (REST API or via the bundled MCP server) are
# managed in the Configuration UI's Secrets tab — entries in `oss/api-keys`
# under USE_API_KEYS=true, and per-tenant under MULTI_TENANT=true. There is
# no env var here for them. Agents (Claude Desktop, Cursor, etc.) and external
# REST clients send their own `x-api-key` header per request; mcp-server
# forwards whatever the connecting agent provides.

# Tap execution isolation (see https://docs.datris.ai/tap-execution-isolation).
# docker compose defaults USE_TAP_RUNNER=true (sidecar). sbt/IDE without the
# sidecar stays in-process. scripts/install.sh and docker/vault-init.sh mint TAP_RUNNER_TOKEN; the
# server refuses to start isolated with an empty or changeme token.
# Set USE_TAP_RUNNER=false to force in-process (loud server warning).
# USE_TAP_RUNNER=true
# TAP_RUNNER_TOKEN=
# TAP_RUNNER_URL=http://datris-tap-runner:8090
# TAP_RUNNER_CALLBACK_HOST=datris

# Optional bundled Ollama service model (only used if you uncomment the
# `ollama` service in docker-compose.yml — disabled by default). This is the
# model Ollama pulls on first start; pick anything available at
# https://ollama.com/library. Once enabled, you can point AI Primary or
# CodeGen at it via the Configuration tab.
# OLLAMA_MODEL=qwen2.5:14b-instruct

# JVM heap sizing for the datris-server container. Default (set in
# docker-compose.yml) is -Xms512m -Xmx2g — fits an 8GB host alongside TEI,
# postgres, mongo, etc. Bump -Xmx on larger hosts if you hit OutOfMemoryError
# during heavy ingestion, embedding batches, or large query results.
# Suggested: 16GB host → -Xmx4g, 24GB → -Xmx8g, 32GB+ → -Xmx12g.
# JAVA_OPTS=-Xms1g -Xmx8g
